<h1>Euler Totient Phi Function</h1>
<hr>
The Euler's Totient Function, denoted as φ(n), is a function that counts the number of positive integers up to n that are relatively prime to n<br>(i.e., numbers that have no common divisors with n other than 1). For example, ϕ(9)=6 because the numbers 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, and 8 are relatively <br>prime to 9.The function has important applications in number theory, particularly in the fields of modular arithmetic and cryptography.This formula <br>utilizes the prime factorization of n to determine the count of integers that are coprime with n.
<b>Implementation:</b>
<div class="a"  >
    int phi( int n )
{
    int ret = n; 
    for ( auto p : prime ) { 
        if ( 1LL * p * p > n || n == 0 ) break;
 
        if (  n % p == 0 ) {
            ret /= p; 
            ret *= (p-1); 
 
            while ( n % p == 0 ) {
                n /= p;
            }
        }
    }
 
    if ( n > 1 ) { 
        ret /= n; 
        ret *= (n-1); 
    }
 
    return ret;
 
}
</div>
